/P 182 0 R
/E (The agenda before this committee -: "Enhancing International Cooperation to Combat Human Trafficking and Migrant Smuggling" addresses one of the most pressing transnational security and humanitarian challenges of the twenty-first century. Every year, millions of individuals become victims of trafficking for purposes including forced labour, sexual exploitation, forced criminality and organ removal, while countless migrants risk dangerous journeys facilitated by sophisticated smuggling networks. These crimes transcend borders, exploit vulnerable populations, generate billions in illicit profits, and continue to )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
232 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (evolve alongside technological advancements and geopolitical instability. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
233 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (Unlike traditional United Nations committees, delegates in INTERPOL represent their respective countries' law enforcement agencies through their National Central Bureaus )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
234 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (\(NCBs\). Consequently, debate should prioritise practical policing strategies, intelligence )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
235 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (sharing, investigative cooperation, victim identification, border management, financial investigations and operational coordination rather than political rhetoric or diplomatic posturing. Delegates are encouraged to approach this committee from the perspective of senior law enforcement officials tasked with dismantling organised criminal enterprises while )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
236 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (respecting international law, national sovereignty and human rights. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
