/S /P
/P 183 0 R
/E (The International Criminal Police Organization \(INTERPOL\) is the world's largest international police organisation, facilitating cross-border cooperation among the law enforcement agencies of its 196 member countries. Established in 1923 as the International Criminal Police Commission, INTERPOL was created to ensure that criminals could not )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
248 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 183 0 R
/E (evade justice simply by crossing national borders. Following organisational restructuring after the Second World War, the organisation adopted its current name and has since expanded into )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
249 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 183 0 R
/E (a global network connecting police authorities across every continent. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
250 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 183 0 R
/E (Contrary to a widespread misconception, INTERPOL is not an international police force. It possesses neither arrest powers nor investigative jurisdiction within sovereign states. Instead, INTERPOL functions as a coordination and information-sharing organisation, enabling national law enforcement agencies to exchange intelligence, coordinate investigations and provide mutual assistance in combating transnational crime. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
251 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 183 0 R
/E (Each member country maintains a National Central Bureau \(NCB\), which acts as the )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
252 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 183 0 R
/E (official point of contact between domestic law enforcement agencies and INTERPOL's General Secretariat in Lyon, France. Through these NCBs, countries exchange intelligence, request operational assistance, and coordinate investigations spanning multiple jurisdictions. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
253 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 183 0 R
/E (The Executive Board \(INTERPOL\) )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
