370 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 192 0 R
/E (Recognising that human trafficking and migrant smuggling cannot be addressed by individual states alone, the international community has developed a comprehensive legal framework to promote cooperation while protecting victims' rights. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
371 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 192 0 R
/E (The cornerstone of this framework is the United Nations Convention against Transnational Organized Crime \(UNTOC\), adopted in 2000. The Convention provides a common legal framework for countries to cooperate in preventing, investigating, and prosecuting transnational organized crime. It is supplemented by two key protocols: the Protocol to Prevent, Suppress and Punish Trafficking in Persons, Especially Women and Children \(Palermo Protocol\) and the Protocol against the Smuggling of Migrants by Land, Sea and Air. Together, these instruments provide internationally recognised definitions of trafficking and smuggling while encouraging States to criminalise these offences, strengthen border management, protect victims and cooperate in cross-border investigations. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
372 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 192 0 R
/E (International organizations also help countries implement these legal frameworks through practical support and cooperation. INTERPOL helps police forces from different countries share information and coordinate investigations. The United Nations Office on Drugs and Crime \(UNODC\) provides training, technical assistance, and support for implementing international laws against organized crime. The International Organization for Migration \(IOM\) works to protect migrants, assist victims of trafficking, and promote safe and orderly migration. Together, these organizations strengthen international cooperation and support countries in preventing, investigating, and responding to human trafficking and migrant smuggling.. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
373 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 192 0 R
/E (Alongside UNTOC and its Protocols, several international human rights treaties reinforce efforts to combat human trafficking and migrant smuggling. The Universal Declaration of Human Rights \(UDHR\) affirms the right to life, liberty, and security and prohibits slavery and servitude. The International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights \(ICCPR\) further prohibits forced labour and arbitrary deprivation of liberty. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
374 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 192 0 R
/E (International Legal and Institutional Framework )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
