377 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 193 0 R
/E (As the world's largest international police organization, INTERPOL plays a vital role in helping its 196 member countries cooperate against human trafficking and migrant smuggling. Since these crimes often involve multiple countries, INTERPOL facilitates the exchange of information, coordination of investigations, and operational support between national law enforcement agencies. )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
378 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 193 0 R
/E (Unlike national police forces, INTERPOL does not have the authority to make arrests or conduct independent investigations. Instead, it supports member countries through tools such as the I-24/7 secure global communication system, which enables real-time intelligence sharing, and the INTERPOL Notices system, which helps locate fugitives, identify victims and missing persons, and support cross-border investigations. )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
379 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 193 0 R
/E (INTERPOL also coordinates multinational operations, provides criminal intelligence and forensic support, delivers specialized training, and works closely with organizations such as UNODC and IOM. By strengthening international police cooperation and intelligence sharing, INTERPOL helps member countries detect, investigate, and dismantle transnational trafficking and migrant smuggling networks. )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
380 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 193 0 R
/E (Despite growing international cooperation, several challenges continue to hinder efforts to combat human trafficking and migrant smuggling. Differences in national laws and legal procedures can make cross-border investigations and extradition difficult. Limited intelligence )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
381 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 193 0 R
/E (Key Challenges to International Cooperation )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
382 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 193 0 R
/E (The Convention on the Rights of the Child \(CRC\) requires States to protect children from all forms of exploitation, while the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination against Women \(CEDAW\) calls upon States to suppress trafficking in women and the exploitation of prostitution. Together, these instruments ensure that  fforts to combat trafficking and smuggling remain grounded in the protection of human rights and the dignity of victims. )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
