388 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 194 0 R
/E (Another major challenge is ensuring that victims are identified and protected. Many victims are mistakenly treated as irregular migrants or offenders, preventing them from accessing the support and justice they need. Addressing these challenges requires stronger international cooperation, greater information sharing, and a victim-centred approach to law enforcement. )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
389 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 194 0 R
/E (Rapid technological advancements have created new challenges for law enforcement. Criminal networks increasingly use encrypted messaging platforms, social media, artificial intelligence, and cryptocurrencies to recruit victims, coordinate operations, and transfer illicit funds. For example, trafficking syndicates have used fake online job advertisements and social media platforms to lure victims with false promises of employment abroad, making detection more difficult. )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
390 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 194 0 R
/E (Humanitarian crises and armed conflicts have also increased the vulnerability of displaced populations. Recent conflicts in Ukraine and Sudan, along with the growth of online scam compounds in Southeast Asia, have demonstrated how criminal networks exploit people fleeing violence or seeking employment. Many victims have been trafficked into forced labour, sexual exploitation, or cyber-enabled scam operations. )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
391 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 194 0 R
/E (Another major challenge is ensuring that victims are identified and protected. Victims are often misidentified as irregular migrants or offenders, preventing them from receiving legal protection, rehabilitation, and access to justice. Addressing these challenges requires stronger international cooperation, timely intelligence sharing, improved use of technology, and a victim-centred approach that places equal importance on prosecuting offenders and protecting those affected. )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
392 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 194 0 R
/Pg 25 0 R
/A [692 0 R 0]
/K [7]
>>
endobj
393 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 195 0 R
/Pg 26 0 R
/A [693 0 R 0]
/K [0]
>>
endobj
394 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 195 0 R
/E (Recent developments show that human trafficking and migrant smuggling are becoming more sophisticated, requiring stronger international cooperation and coordinated law enforcement responses. )
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
395 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 195 0 R
/E (One significant trend is the rise of technology enabled trafficking. Criminal networks increasingly use social media, encrypted messaging applications, and fake online job advertisements to recruit victims across borders. Artificial intelligence and deepfake technology have also been used to create false identities and fraudulent documents, making investigations more complex. )
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
396 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 195 0 R
/E (Another major concern is the emergence of online scam compounds in Southeast Asia, particularly in parts of Myanmar, Cambodia, and Laos. Thousands of individuals have been trafficked under the false promise of legitimate employment and forced to operate online investment, cryptocurrency, and romance scams. These cases highlight the growing connection between human trafficking, cybercrime, and transnational organized crime. )
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
397 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 195 0 R
/E (Ongoing humanitarian crises have also heightened the risk of trafficking. Conflicts in Ukraine, Sudan, and parts of the Middle East have displaced millions of people, leaving many vulnerable to exploitation, forced labour, and sexual trafficking. Criminal networks often target displaced persons who lack financial resources, legal documentation, or access to protection. )
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
398 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 195 0 R
/E (In response to these evolving threats, INTERPOL, in collaboration with member countries and international organizations such as UNODC and IOM, has coordinated multinational operations to identify victims, dismantle trafficking networks, and strengthen intelligence sharing. These developments highlight the importance of international cooperation, information exchange, and coordinated law enforcement efforts in addressing human trafficking and migrant smuggling. )
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
